---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Whats my ip, and show my ip, both mean the public IP address this connection presents. Find
      my public IP here as IPv4 and, when the path has it, IPv6. They are two families, not two
      writings of the same number. Copy my ipv4 for v4-only forms and allowlists. Copy my ipv6 when
      the peer is v6-first. Your browser asks an IP echo for the number. ToyTools never sees the
      reply.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">See What Is My IP →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-a-lookup-works">
    <h2>How a Public IP Lookup Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A static page cannot know your public IP address by itself. The address is assigned on the
      path out, by a router, a carrier NAT, or a VPN. The only way to read it is to ask a host on
      the other side what it saw. This page does that from your browser, toward ipify.org, and
      prints the reply. Nothing is stored here, and ToyTools is not in the middle of the request.
      Therefore a blocker that stops ipify will make the lookup fail. A VPN changes the number
      because the echo sees the egress, not your living room.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      <strong>A public IPv4.</strong> For example, 203.0.113.10 is a public IP address. Paste that
      dotted string into a security group that asks for IPv4.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Dual stack.</strong> For example, the same session can also show 2001:db8::1. That is
      my ipv6, not a rewriting of my ipv4. An allowlist of one will not match the other.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>CGNAT.</strong> For example, 100.64.1.20 sits in 100.64.0.0/10. Treat it as shared,
      not as a public IP versus CGNAT mix-up you can whitelist.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="ipv4-and-ipv6">
    <h2>IPv4 and IPv6 on the Same Path <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The difference between IPv4 and IPv6 is the address family, not the spelling. IPv4 versus
      IPv6 on the same path is dual-stack: both are true at once. An IPv4 allowlist will not match
      an IPv6 packet, and a v4-only form will reject a v6 address even when both are correct for
      this connection. Try the other family before assuming the lookup failed. A v6-only path has
      no IPv4 to copy, which is a fact rather than a bug.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cgnat">
    <h2>When the Address Is CGNAT <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      100.64.0.0/10 is shared address space for carrier-grade NAT, RFC 6598. Public IP versus CGNAT
      is the reachability test: a 100.64 address is not globally reachable. Opening a port on it,
      or whitelisting it on a cloud host, will not match traffic from the public internet. The same
      is true of RFC 1918 private ranges (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16) if an echo ever returned
      one. That usually means the lookup saw a local or VPN-internal address rather than the
      public egress.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Copying the family the form does not accept">
      <p>
        IPv4 and IPv6 are both "your IP". A security group that wants dotted IPv4 will reject the
        v6 string. Copy the labelled family, not whichever number sat higher on the page.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Whitelisting a 100.64 address">
      <p>
        CGNAT addresses are shared and not dialable from the public internet. If you need inbound
        reachability, that is a public IP or a tunnel, not a 100.64 allowlist.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="The echo sees the VPN">
      <p>
        If a home whitelist still fails, look up again with the VPN off. The number this page shows
        is the egress the remote host sees, which is the VPN when it is on.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
